[{"id": "question 6151", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "SCC AS Restoration (TS 24.237) primarily targets continuity/restoration of session/registration state after SCC AS restart including HSS information interaction; whether it is also formally described as 'ensuring SRVCC' as a top-level purpose is not clearly verifiable from accessible 3GPP excerpts.", "source": "TS 24.237 / 23.237 (per 3GPP Release 14 SCC AS Restoration work item)"}, {"id": "question 6216", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "TS 23.502 §4.4.1.2 explicitly states: 'The SMF sends an N4 session establishment request message to the UPF that contains the structured control information which defines how the UPF needs to behave.'", "source": "3GPP TS 23.502 §4.4.1.2 (N4 Session Establishment)"}, {"id": "question 6239", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "5GS only supports access via E-UTRA and NR; GERAN UEs register in 2G/3G core, never in 5GC. Access to a 5GC via GERAN is not defined in 3GPP architecture.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.501 §5.16 (services not supported by 5GS)"}, {"id": "question 6416", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "At MgNB handover, the source gNB's PDCP entity is released and the target gNB re-establishes a PDCP entity; whether packet duplication is 'dropped' (option 4), 'deactivated' (option 1), or 'retained' depends on configuration - the precise normative statement is not clearly verifiable from accessible excerpts.", "source": "TS 37.340 §10 (PDCP duplication at handover)"}, {"id": "question 6510", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "ETSI GS MEC 003 IaaS deployment model: the IaaS provider facilitates management tools so the MEC orchestrator (owned by the service provider, tenant of MEC infrastructure) can interact with VIM/virtualization underneath. None of the options cleanly captures this role.", "source": "ETSI GS MEC 003 (IaaS deployment); ARXIV 1803.07009 'Multi-access Edge Computing: the driver behind the wheel of 5G-connected cars'"}, {"id": "question 6596", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "TS 32.240 §4.4.2.2: 'The CAP reference point provides similar functionality for online charging as Ro, however, it is based on CAMEL techniques.' (Gy §4.4.2.3 in contrast is 'functionally equivalent to Ro').", "source": "3GPP TS 32.240 §4.4.2.2 (Rel-18)"}, {"id": "question 6597", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "MPS per TS 22.153/23.854 spans IMS, CS, and PS domains; there is no formal 'primarily PS domain' definition - the question's 'primarily' qualifier is underspecified.", "source": "3GPP TS 22.153, TR 23.854"}, {"id": "question 6602", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "TS 38.213/38.211: Random Access procedure supports initial access, handover, beam failure recovery, and - when no dedicated PUCCH SR is configured - scheduling request transmission via RACH.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.213, TS 38.211"}, {"id": "question 6612", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "TS 23.548 / 3GPP S2-2211366: 'if AMF 322 receives the HR-SBO permission indication for the requested DNN/S-NSSAI..., the AMF 322 selects V-SMF 328 supporting HR-SBO' - AMF and V-SMF are the defining network-side entities for HR-SBO PDU session establishment.", "source": "TS 23.548 (HR-SBO); patent CN120380786A"}, {"id": "question 6633", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "TS 24.501 5GMM cause codes include #37 '5GS services not allowed', which is returned when the MS is on a non-CAG cell of a CAG-only PLMN where it can only access 5GS via CAG cells.", "source": "3GPP TS 24.501 (5GMM cause #37); TS 23.501 §5.30 CAG"}, {"id": "question 6856", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "Per TS 23.502/NGAP, the AMF is the network function that applies priority treatment for Initial UE Messages during registration based on subscription and the priority indication conveyed by (R)AN. The marked 'AMF' interpretation for 'provides priority handling' is consistent with AMF processing responsibility.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.502 §4.2.2.2; TS 38.413 §9.2.5.1"}, {"id": "question 6935", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "Historically cross-over Ethernet cables were required for like-device connections (PC-PC and switch-switch), NOT for PC-switch/hub (straight-through). Modern auto-MDI/MDIX makes cables interchangeable, but a strict reading makes option 4 'All of the above' include a false case (option 2). No single option captures both legitimate cases.", "source": "IEEE 802.3 (auto-MDI/MDIX history); general Ethernet cabling convention"}, {"id": "question 6945", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Per IEEE 802.11-2020 §23.3.12.2.4.2 (NDP_1M Ack), when Idle Indication=1 the Duration field is set to the sleep/wakeup duration for the STA being acknowledged - effectively the 'duration of time expected from the STA that elicited the response'.", "source": "IEEE 802.11-2020 §23.3.12.2.4.2 (S1G NDP_1M Ack)"}, {"id": "question 6991", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "TS 22.153 / TS 23.854: in the PS domain, when the requested MPS service is not supported, MPS priority treatment falls back to voice services as the highest priority basic service.", "source": "3GPP TS 22.153; TR 23.854"}, {"id": "question 7063", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per TS 23.237 / TS 24.237, after SRVCC/VCC the held call is re-established in the IMS domain rather than being terminated or transferred to a third party.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.237 / TS 24.237 (IMS Service Continuity)"}, {"id": "question 7252", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "TS 32.240 §4.3.1.1: 'The CTF is a mandatory, integrated component in all network elements that provide offline charging functionality... the Account Metrics Collection can be considered as the network element dependent part of the CTF.' CTF lives in the Network Element, not in UE.", "source": "3GPP TS 32.240 §4.3.1.1"}, {"id": "question 7330", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "TS 38.401 §3.1: gNB-CU is a logical node hosting RRC/SDAP/PDCP and controls gNB-DUs; it terminates F1 (to DU) and - via its CU-CP/CU-UP split - the Xn interface between gNBs. Connecting gNBs to the 5GC is the overall gNB's purpose, not specifically the gNB-CU; 'interconnect gNBs through the Xn interface' is a defining gNB-CU role.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.401 §3.1"}, {"id": "question 7385", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "3GPP Shared Network specifications (TS 22.951 / TS 23.501): the Hosting E-UTRAN/NG-RAN Operator can control resource usage for signaling traffic and user traffic independently, including per Participating Operator allocation.", "source": "3GPP TS 22.951 (Service aspects of shared network)"}]